    "summary": "Complete exam-ready guide to Post-Gupta Period.",
    "body": "<h3>Part 1: What Was the Post-Gupta Period?</h3>\n<p>After the Gupta Empire declined in 550 AD, India was divided into many small kingdoms. This period is called the Post-Gupta Period. It lasted from 550 AD to 750 AD.</p>\n<p>Main kingdoms: Harshavardhana (606 AD - 647 AD), Chalukya Dynasty (6th - 12th Century AD), Pallava Dynasty (4th - 9th Century AD).</p>\n<h3>Part 2: Harshavardhana (606 AD - 647 AD)</h3>\n<p>Capital: Kannauj.</p>\n<p>Biography: Harshacharita by Banabhatta.</p>\n<p>Religion: Initially Shaiva, later became Buddhist.</p>\n<p>Chinese Traveler: Hiuen Tsang (Xuanzang).</p>\n<p>Death: 647 AD.</p>\n<p>Significance: United North India for a short time.</p>\n<h3>Part 3: Chalukya Dynasty (6th - 12th Century AD)</h3>\n<p>Founder: Pulakeshin I.</p>\n<p>Capital: Badami (Vatapi).</p>\n<p>Greatest Ruler: Pulakeshin II.</p>\n<p>Achievements: Defeated Harshavardhana.</p>\n<p>Art: Chalukya temples at Aihole, Badami, Pattadakal.</p>\n<p>Significance: Great patrons of art and architecture.</p>\n<h3>Part 4: Pallava Dynasty (4th - 9th Century AD)</h3>\n<p>Capital: Kanchipuram.</p>\n<p>Greatest Ruler: Narasimhavarman I.</p>\n<p>Achievements: Defeated Pulakeshin II.</p>\n<p>Art: Mahabalipuram (Shore Temple, Pancha Rathas).</p>\n<p>Script: Pallava script (origin of South Indian scripts).</p>\n<p>Significance: Great patrons of art and architecture.</p>\n<h3>Part 5: Hiuen Tsang (Xuanzang)</h3>\n<p>Who: Chinese Buddhist monk and traveler.</p>\n<p>Time: 7th Century AD.</p>\n<p>Visited: During Harshavardhana's reign.</p>\n<p>Book: Si-Yu-Ki (Record of the Western Regions).</p>\n<p>Significance: Described India's society, religion, and administration.</p>\n<h3>Part 6: Important Facts for SSC</h3>\n<p>Harshavardhana's Capital | Kannauj</p>\n<p>Harshavardhana's Biography | Harshacharita by Banabhatta</p>\n<p>Chinese Traveler | Hiuen Tsang (Xuanzang)</p>\n<p>Hiuen Tsang's Book | Si-Yu-Ki</p>\n<p>Harshavardhana's Religion | Initially Shaiva, later Buddhist</p>\n<p>Harshavardhana's Death | 647 AD</p>\n<p>Founder of Chalukya Dynasty | Pulakeshin I</p>\n<p>Chalukya Capital | Badami (Vatapi)</p>\n<p>Greatest Chalukya Ruler | Pulakeshin II</p>\n<p>Pulakeshin II Defeated | Harshavardhana</p>\n<p>Chalukya Temples | Aihole, Badami, Pattadakal</p>\n<p>Pallava Capital | Kanchipuram</p>\n<p>Greatest Pallava Ruler | Narasimhavarman I</p>\n<p>Narasimhavarman I Defeated | Pulakeshin II</p>\n<p>Pallava Monuments | Mahabalipuram (Shore Temple, Pancha Rathas)</p>\n<p>Pallava Script | Origin of South Indian scripts</p>\n<p><b>Q1: Who was the ruler of Kannauj during the Post-Gupta period? Answer: Harshavardhana</b></p>\n<p><b>Q2: Who wrote Harshacharita? Answer: Banabhatta</b></p>\n<p><b>Q3: Which Chinese traveler visited India during Harshavardhana's reign? Answer: Hiuen Tsang (Xuanzang)</b></p>\n<p><b>Q4: Which book did Hiuen Tsang write? Answer: Si-Yu-Ki</b></p>\n<p><b>Q5: Who was the founder of the Chalukya Dynasty? Answer: Pulakeshin I</b></p>\n<p><b>Q6: What was the capital of the Chalukyas? Answer: Badami (Vatapi)</b></p>\n<p><b>Q7: Who was the greatest Chalukya ruler? Answer: Pulakeshin II</b></p>\n<p><b>Q8: Who defeated Harshavardhana? Answer: Pulakeshin II</b></p>\n<p><b>Q9: What was the capital of the Pallavas? Answer: Kanchipuram</b></p>\n<p><b>Q10: Who was the greatest Pallava ruler? Answer: Narasimhavarman I</b></p>\n<p><b>Q11: Who defeated Pulakeshin II? Answer: Narasimhavarman I</b></p>\n<p><b>Q12: Which Pallava monuments are famous? Answer: Mahabalipuram (Shore Temple, Pancha Rathas)</b></p>\n<p><b>Q13: Which script is the origin of South Indian scripts? Answer: Pallava script</b></p>\n<p><b>Q14: When did Harshavardhana die? Answer: 647 AD</b></p>\n<p><b>Q15: Which temples were built by the Chalukyas? Answer: Aihole, Badami, Pattadakal</b></p>"
